=== 1.1 STRUCTURE ===
Rectangular table: 28333 rows x 483 columns (SPSS .sav with value labels)
Columns with data in all 24 countries: 67
Untidy layout ('column headers are values'): 152 d_* columns hold one variable per COUNTRY,
  e.g. education is spread over 27 columns (24 main + Germany/Israel supplementary); each row has at most one non-empty d_educ_* main column
  check: rows with >1 main education column filled = 0
Missing values are stored as numeric sentinel codes (8, 9, 98, 99), not as blanks; left as-is they would be read as real, very large answers
  sentinel codes found via value labels: {'ai_heard': [8, 9], 'ai_cncexc': [8, 9], 'age': [98, 99], 'd_educ_uk': [98, 99]}
Q32 code 8 is labelled 'Not sure' (a real answer), so a blanket 'recode 8 to missing' would be wrong

=== 1.2 GRANULARITY ===
One row = one respondent (individual level).
Primary key (country, id) unique: True; id unique on its own: True
Weights differ by row (range 0.061-5.89, mean 1.00 within every country): each row stands for a different number of people; each country's weights sum to its sample size, NOT to its population (India n = 3,545 would count 3.5x a typical country if countries are pooled)

=== 1.3 SCOPE ===
Rows in file vs Pew's published sample sizes ("how many rows should be here?"): total 28333 vs 28333; countries with a mismatch: none
24 countries; the U.S. (part of Pew's 25-country AI report) is a separate survey and is NOT in this file
Population: adults 18+ only; national samples, but 'national' differs (see Pew methodology for coverage limits)
Enough data for the question? respondents aged 18-29 per country (smallest five):
  Hungary 80, South Korea 80, Greece 98, Canada 102, Netherlands 107
Not enough for some questions: Australia has no exact age (bands only) and no internet use; ideology not asked in India, Indonesia, Kenya

=== 1.4 TEMPORALITY ===
Single wave: 'survey' = ['Spring, 2025']; the file has NO interview-date variable
Fieldwork (from Pew methodology, outside the file): Jan 8 - Apr 26 2025; window differs by country, e.g.
  Indonesia Jan 8-Feb 23 | Turkey Jan 31-Mar 3 | Australia Mar 17-31 | Italy Feb 7-Apr 26
Implication: opinions on a fast-moving technology are a snapshot of early 2025; cross-country differences may partly reflect timing

=== 1.5 FAITHFULNESS ===
Values outside the codebook (unrealistic / 'incorrect' values):
  ai_heard             none
  ai_cncexc            none
  aireg_us             none
  aireg_china          none
  aireg_eu             none
  aireg_country        none
  also checked (gender, gender_australia, age_australia, internetfreq, political_scale2/3, 24 education columns): any problem would be listed above
Age: valid range 18-97; 97 is TOP-CODED ('97 or older'), so not an exact age; n aged 97+ = 3
Duplicated answer patterns (all columns except id) = 0 -> no sign of copied/fabricated records
Large weights (outliers): largest per country, top 3: Australia 5.89, Italy 4.71, Poland 4.19
Design effects from Pew (variance inflation from weighting/clustering): India 3.2, Kenya 2.44, Indonesia 2.23, Australia 1.21, Germany 1.33
Survey mode differs (telephone / face-to-face / online panel), which can change how often people say 'don't know':
  Q31 DK/Refused by mode: Face-to-face 13.0%, Online panel 0.3%, Telephone 1.7%

